  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Total federal contract obligations in Maryland are $25.9B for FY2026 to date. Obligations fell 34.8% year over year. Award activity includes 72,600 awards with an average award size of $356K. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer in the state with obligations of $10.5B. The Department of Health and Human Services follows with obligations of $5.1B. Research and development work leads industry obligations at $2.9B. Engineering Services, Computer Systems Design Services, and Other Computer Related Services each account for $2.7B in obligations. The Johns Hopkins University Applied Physics Laboratory LLC is the top vendor with $1.5B in obligations. ORANO FEDERAL SERVICES LLC and AMERICAN CENTRIFUGE OPERATING, LLC each have $900M in obligations. FY25 obligations were $39.7B, and FY24 obligations were $46.3B, showing that current-year totals are partial because FY2026 is fiscal year to date. Readers should treat FY2026 totals as incomplete because the fiscal year is still in progress.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What changed in FY2026 federal contracting in Maryland?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 34.8% year over year. The statewide total for FY2026 to date is $25.9B, down from FY25 obligations of $39.7B. Award volume remains substantial at 72,600 awards with an average award size of $356K. The drop in obligations coincides with lower current-year totals since FY2026 is fiscal year to date."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who are the biggest buyers and vendors in Maryland right now?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $10.5B, and the Department of Health and Human Services obligated $5.1B. THE JOHNS HOPKINS UNIVERSITY APPLIED PHYSICS LABORATORY LLC received $1.5B, and ORANO FEDERAL SERVICES LLC received $900M. Key industries with large obligations include Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences at $2.9B and Engineering Services at $2.7B."
      }
    ]
  },
